Q: Making sure Enum prints as a number A: <p>One option is to use named constants instead of enums. Something like:</p>
<pre><code>public static class Departments
{
    public const int Accounting = 1;
    public const int Sales = 2;
    public const int Security = 3;
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not allowed to redesign the database, obviously, even if that seems like a good idea in this slightly simplified example</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not being able to change the database is a problem, and the recommended practices is to have some way to apply schema changes to the database so it can evolve together with your application. See Entity framework migrations for an example on how this could work, but there are other possible approaches to the same problem. This should allow you do modify the database, and should allow you to change the column to an actual enum column if that is supported by your database.</p>

Q: iOS26 SwiftUI DatePicker giving unreliable/inconsistent date formatting A: <p>This certainly looks like a bug. I noticed that it happens more often when the list of dates is scrolled quickly. I suspect that it may be some kind of timing issue in the way the compact date picker style goes about preparing the formatted date.</p>
<p>A workaround is to factor out the picker into a separate view and then force a refresh when it becomes visible. Changing the <code>displayedComponents</code> is one way to do this. I found that the change needs to be performed asynchronously using <code>.task</code>, doing it in <code>.onAppear</code> didn't help.</p>
<p>Here is how the picker can be factored out into a separate view:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct DatePickerWithRefresh: View {
    @Binding var date: Date
    @State private var isShowing = false

    var body: some View {
        DatePicker(
            &quot;Date &amp; Time&quot;,
            selection: $date,
            displayedComponents: isShowing ? [.date, .hourAndMinute] : [.hourAndMinute]
        )
        .labelsHidden()
        .datePickerStyle(.compact)
        .task { isShowing = true }
        .onDisappear { isShowing = false }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And here is how it is plugged into your example:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>// RosterImportView

ForEach($pdfImportCoordinator.arrivalSegments) { $segment in
    HStack {
        TextField(&quot;Airport&quot;, text: $segment.airport)
            // + modifiers, as before

        DatePickerWithRefresh(date: $segment.date)
            .padding()
    }
    .listRowBackground(Color.clear)
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to create a type-deducing tuple with neither copyable nor movable members? The tuple itself is a class member A: <p>You might define the prototype of <code>MakeContainerOfS</code> as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Ts&gt;
auto MakeContainerOfS(std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt;) -&gt; std::tuple&lt;S&lt;Ts&gt;...&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that the function doesn't need to have a body, we are just interested in the result type. We just provide as arguments the types of the input tuple to the <code>S</code> template class. In other words, we just work with types and not objects here, which is required since <code>S</code> objects can't be copied.</p>
<p>Then you can define <code>Container</code> like this:</p>
<pre><code>struct Container {

    static constexpr std::tuple t = std::make_tuple (1, 2.0f);

    decltype(MakeContainerOfS(t)) container = t;

    void Call() {
        std::apply([](auto &amp;...members) { (members.InterfaceMethod(), ...); }, container);
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>where you define a tuple <code>t</code> holding the values of interest. Note that we don't have to use explicitely <code>std::tuple&lt;int,float&gt;</code>, we let the compiler deduce the types for us.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bs471n5ce" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>It is possible to make <code>Container</code> a little bit more generic by providing the values as non type template parameters (in case the values you want to use can be used as NTTP):</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;auto...values&gt;
struct Container {

    static constexpr std::tuple t = std::make_tuple (values...);

    decltype(MakeContainerOfS(t)) container = t;

    void Call() {
        std::apply([](auto &amp;...members) { (members.InterfaceMethod(), ...); }, container);
    }
};

int main() {
    Container&lt;1,2.0f&gt;{}.Call();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/cz5a8bhcn" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Update</strong></p>
<p>Suppose one needs to provide more information to the <code>S</code> constructor, e.g.</p>
<pre><code>S(T v, int n, const char* s) : t(v) {}
</code></pre>
<p>We can put all the information (<code>T</code> value + an <code>int</code> and a <code>const char*</code>) into a tuple like this:</p>
<pre><code>static constexpr std::tuple info = std::make_tuple (
    std::make_tuple(1,   5, &quot;five&quot; ),
    std::make_tuple(2.0f,6, &quot;six&quot;  ),
    std::make_tuple('a', 7, &quot;seven&quot;)
);
</code></pre>
<p>and modify the code above as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;std::size_t Idx, typename...Ts&gt;
auto MakeContainerOfS(std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt;) -&gt; std::tuple&lt;S&lt;std::tuple_element_t&lt;Idx,Ts&gt;&gt;...&gt;;

struct Container {

    // Tuple holding the values for T + other values.
    static constexpr std::tuple info = std::make_tuple (
        std::make_tuple(1,   5, &quot;five&quot; ),
        std::make_tuple(2.0f,6, &quot;six&quot;  ),
        std::make_tuple('a', 7, &quot;seven&quot;)
    );

    // Create the container whose type is retrieved from 'MakeContainerOfS'
    decltype(MakeContainerOfS&lt;0&gt;(info)) container = info;

    void Call() {
        std::apply([](auto &amp;...members) { (members.InterfaceMethod(), ...); }, container);
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>So, we need a constructor for <code>S</code> that takes a <code>std::tuple</code> as input and delegates to <code>S(T v, int n, const char* s)</code>. This can be done in two steps:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Ts,std::size_t...Is&gt;
S(std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt; params, std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;) : S(std::get&lt;Is&gt;(params)...) {}

template&lt;typename...Ts&gt;
S(std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt; params) : S(params,std::index_sequence_for&lt;Ts...&gt;()) {}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/vT1f5nKG1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
